 {:sender "Psyche Flow Secondary" :context "flow f5a6e9 flow: What is sent to flows are typed requests, not letters; Flow holds no messages; Message asks Flow for a time-bo (2026-10-07)" :verbatim "The abstractions that are sent in flows are not letters. We are going to have actual types. If, let's say, something asked for a certain flow to be compacted, then the logic for how this would unfold, or how that metaflow would be refreshed in terms of routing, would be very different than a message.\n\nObviously if the flow is brand new, the last thing we want to do is compact it. We wouldn't send whatever needs to be sent into that pane for it to compact to the new flow. That's just one example. There's probably a bunch of different behaviors that would correspond to different requests that are being asked to be sent to a certain flow.\n\nI don't think that it's Flow's job to hold messages. It's more like it's Flow's job to tell the message component later on to give it the signal that a message can now be sent. The message is going to ask for a lock, which should be time-bound so that it doesn't lock forever. If it does get a lock then the message nexus can send that object over to Flow with the message because now it has the lock. Flow told the message that it has the lock for that flow or that metaflow. The message doesn't have to know about the flows. It can also just talk in terms of metaflows. And indeed when this is done and more polished, I'm mostly going to talk in terms of meta flows."}
 {:sender "Psyche Flow Secondary" :context "flow 445410 flow: Topics talk at the same layer across aspects (2026-10-09)" :verbatim "Somebody should keep the core psyche, and the core psyche keeps track of all of the psyche within psyche. The topics can essentially talk at the same layer, right? If there's a psyche ethos secondary and there's a mind ethos secondary (meaning there's a mind flow with the ethos topic), they can start talking to each other in terms of implementation because the mind is implementing, but the psyche is seeing. When the psyche gets updated, if we're in the development mode like now, then the mind of the same topic can actually communicate with the same layer and in a shared topic. And this is true of the field, though. The field should go through the mind and not to the psyche directly unless the psyche just spoke to the field. This should be universal in terms of the aspects. Let's put this in some more aspect skill proposal for the vision."}
 {:sender "Psyche Flow Secondary" :context "flow 9fed42 flow: Same topic, same layer, across aspects (2026-10-09)" :verbatim "Somebody should keep the core psyche, and the core psyche keeps track of all of the psyche within psyche. The topics can essentially talk at the same layer, right? If there's a psyche ethos secondary and there's a mind ethos secondary (meaning there's a mind flow with the ethos topic), they can start talking to each other in terms of implementation because the mind is implementing, but the psyche is seeing. When the psyche gets updated, if we're in the development mode like now, then the mind of the same topic can actually communicate with the same layer and in a shared topic. And this is true of the field, though. The field should go through the mind and not to the psyche directly unless the psyche just spoke to the field. This should be universal in terms of the aspects. ... Let's start just implementing them even before I comment, or modifying when I comment, etc."}
 {:sender "Psyche Flow Secondary" :context "flow 875960 flow: Flow keeps the lock and delivers; Message asks Flow to send to a metaflow and subscribes to its events (2026-10-11)" :verbatim "I've actually changed my mind on this. I don't think that the flow should give the lock to message because we need the flow nexus to be the component that delivers the payload because it owns the lock. It can't really give a lock because then, even if it has a timeout, there would be a problematic window when we can't rely on two components sharing a lock. If message wants to send a message, it communicates to flow and flow would take care of the mechanics and then tell message the status. It would also tell message if queued messages have been delivered because a waking message came in and so on. It would just give message the information on what's happening from its perspective. Message would just say, \"Okay I want to send a message to this Metaflow.\" That's what it would tell flow and then flow would do what it does and then respond to message. It might just be like, \"Messages queued.\" Let's say the flow is being refreshed, so the messages are queued for when the flow has restarted. It would have a variant just to express that. Once the message got delivered, message would subscribe to events from flow. When the message gets delivered, message would receive a notification that the message has now entered the context of that flow."}
